1
00:00:00,530 --> 00:00:03,483
At ten minutes past seven on the evening of November 10, 1975,

2
00:00:03,503 --> 00:00:07,510
the captain of the Edmund Fitzgerald answered a radio call from the ship behind him.

3
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:14,650
"We are holding our own."

4
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:17,985
Ten minutes later, the Fitzgerald was gone.

5
00:00:18,055 --> 00:00:19,350
No mayday.

6
00:00:19,420 --> 00:00:21,160
No distress call.

7
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:25,004
29 men, and the biggest ship ever lost on the Great Lakes,

8
00:00:25,024 --> 00:00:28,210
vanished from the radar in the middle of a storm.

9
00:00:28,630 --> 00:00:29,430
Now…

10
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,980
more than 100 miles to the northwest, in the middle of Lake Superior,

11
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,480
where the water should be hundreds of feet deep, there is a mountain.

12
00:00:37,870 --> 00:00:43,290
Its highest peak stops just 21 feet below the surface.

13
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:49,850
Fully loaded, the Edmund Fitzgerald sat about 25 feet deep in the water.

14
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:53,000
One engineer believes that's not a coincidence.

15
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:57,861
He believes the Fitzgerald hit that mountain on the morning of her last day,

16
00:00:57,881 --> 00:00:59,950
nearly ten hours before she sank.

17
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:01,210
And if he's right…

18
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,360
she wasn't the first ship it took.

19
00:01:04,430 --> 00:01:10,070
Tonight, every ship linked to this mountain, and the evidence for it and against it.

20
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:15,250
And before this is over, I'll show you the one place where the proof could still be…

21
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:17,920
and why no one is allowed to touch it.

22
00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:20,240
This is Great Lakes Files.

23
00:01:20,310 --> 00:01:24,960
We go looking for the places hidden in and under these lakes.

24
00:01:25,030 --> 00:01:30,430
And here's the thing: most of the people watching these videos haven't subscribed yet.

25
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:34,280
If you like stories like this, tap subscribe right now.

26
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:40,078
It's free, it takes one second, and it's what keeps this channel going.

27
00:01:40,148 --> 00:01:41,638
It's called Superior Shoal.

28
00:01:41,708 --> 00:01:44,615
It sits about 50 miles north of Copper Harbor,

29
00:01:44,635 --> 00:01:48,688
at the tip of Michigan's Keweenaw Peninsula, far from any shore.

30
00:01:50,208 --> 00:01:52,728
It covers about 20 square miles.

31
00:01:52,798 --> 00:01:54,282
It's made of ancient lava,

32
00:01:54,302 --> 00:01:59,778
from a time more than a billion years ago when this part of North America tried to split apart.

33
00:02:00,048 --> 00:02:04,938
Four ridges run across it, and a deep gorge cuts between them.

34
00:02:05,008 --> 00:02:08,268
Most of it lies tens or hundreds of feet down.

35
00:02:08,338 --> 00:02:11,568
But a few sharp peaks rise almost to the surface.

36
00:02:11,638 --> 00:02:14,318
Fishermen knew something was out there.

37
00:02:14,388 --> 00:02:18,318
Lake trout gather on it, swimming up and down its slopes.

38
00:02:18,388 --> 00:02:21,788
But for a long time it wasn't on any official chart.

39
00:02:21,858 --> 00:02:28,138
Ships crossed this part of the lake believing the water beneath them was hundreds of feet deep.

40
00:02:28,208 --> 00:02:33,508
The United States Lake Survey didn't chart it until 1929.

41
00:02:33,578 --> 00:02:40,558
Which means that two of the strangest disappearances in the history of Lake Superior happened while the mountain was still a secret.

42
00:02:43,257 --> 00:02:46,267
November 21, 1902.

43
00:02:46,337 --> 00:02:49,092
The Bannockburn, a Canadian steel freighter,

44
00:02:49,112 --> 00:02:53,317
was heading east across Lake Superior with 85,000 bushels of wheat.

45
00:02:54,267 --> 00:03:00,217
Her captain, George Wood, was thirty-seven, and he was the oldest man on board.

46
00:03:00,287 --> 00:03:02,987
Most of his crew were seventeen to twenty.

47
00:03:03,057 --> 00:03:05,707
One of the men at the wheel was sixteen.

48
00:03:05,777 --> 00:03:08,581
Around noon, Captain James McMaugh of another freighter, the Algonquin,

49
00:03:08,601 --> 00:03:11,484
saw her through his binoculars, about 7 miles away, out in the open lake,

50
00:03:11,504 --> 00:03:12,757
about 40 miles from Isle Royale.

51
00:03:20,037 --> 00:03:21,557
He knew the ship well.

52
00:03:21,627 --> 00:03:24,697
He watched her several times over a few minutes.

53
00:03:24,767 --> 00:03:28,057
She was steaming along, right on course.

54
00:03:28,127 --> 00:03:30,527
Then he raised his binoculars again…

55
00:03:30,597 --> 00:03:33,147
and she was gone.

56
00:03:33,217 --> 00:03:36,607
He put it down to the haze, and thought no more of it.

57
00:03:36,677 --> 00:03:39,197
A storm hit the lake that night.

58
00:03:39,267 --> 00:03:42,947
The Bannockburn never reached the locks at Sault Ste. Marie.

59
00:03:43,017 --> 00:03:47,187
That night, the crew of a passenger steamer passed a ship in the storm.

60
00:03:47,257 --> 00:03:50,797
They thought her lights looked like the Bannockburn's.

61
00:03:50,867 --> 00:03:54,987
If it was her, it was the last time anyone saw her.

62
00:03:55,057 --> 00:03:57,417
For days, families waited.

63
00:03:57,487 --> 00:04:01,647
One manager even told a newspaper she'd been found, crew safe.

64
00:04:01,717 --> 00:04:03,987
It wasn't true.

65
00:04:04,057 --> 00:04:04,837
In December,

66
00:04:04,857 --> 00:04:10,437
a single cork life jacket from the Bannockburn washed up on a beach at Grand Marais,

67
00:04:10,457 --> 00:04:11,037
Michigan.

68
00:04:11,497 --> 00:04:13,817
It's the only piece of her ever found.

69
00:04:13,887 --> 00:04:14,927
No wreck.

70
00:04:14,997 --> 00:04:16,637
No bodies.

71
00:04:16,707 --> 00:04:21,147
Within a year, sailors were calling her the Flying Dutchman of the Great Lakes.

72
00:04:21,217 --> 00:04:23,767
A ghost ship.

73
00:04:23,837 --> 00:04:25,067
There are plenty of theories.

74
00:04:25,137 --> 00:04:26,547
A boiler explosion.

75
00:04:26,617 --> 00:04:29,827
A reef near Caribou Island.

76
00:04:29,897 --> 00:04:34,287
But one theory points back to the open water where she was last seen…

77
00:04:34,357 --> 00:04:40,828
and to a mountain that, in 1902, was on no chart at all.

78
00:04:40,898 --> 00:04:44,878
Sixteen years later, the mountain may have taken warships.

79
00:04:44,948 --> 00:04:48,072
In 1918, at Fort William in Ontario, now Thunder Bay,

80
00:04:48,092 --> 00:04:51,928
a Canadian factory built twelve minesweepers for the French Navy.

81
00:04:54,198 --> 00:04:58,998
Each was 135 feet long, with two deck guns.

82
00:04:59,068 --> 00:05:02,948
Their job was to clear mines off the coast of France.

83
00:05:03,018 --> 00:05:06,818
By the time they were ready, the First World War was over.

84
00:05:06,888 --> 00:05:09,928
But the ships still had to get to France.

85
00:05:09,998 --> 00:05:11,155
So in late November,

86
00:05:11,175 --> 00:05:15,155
three of them set out together across Lake Superior: the Sebastopol,

87
00:05:15,175 --> 00:05:16,978
the Inkerman and the Cerisoles.

88
00:05:19,558 --> 00:05:23,188
On the 24th, a storm caught them on the open lake.

89
00:05:23,258 --> 00:05:25,478
The Sebastopol made it through.

90
00:05:25,548 --> 00:05:29,028
The Inkerman and the Cerisoles did not.

91
00:05:29,098 --> 00:05:30,998
They simply never arrived.

92
00:05:31,068 --> 00:05:36,528
78 men were lost, the worst loss of life Lake Superior has ever seen.

93
00:05:36,598 --> 00:05:40,598
And they were the last warships ever to sink in the Great Lakes.

94
00:05:40,668 --> 00:05:44,318
More than a hundred years later, no wreckage has ever been found.

95
00:05:44,388 --> 00:05:46,268
Not a lifeboat.

96
00:05:46,338 --> 00:05:48,308
Not a plank.

97
00:05:48,378 --> 00:05:51,680
Their route to the locks crossed the middle of the lake,

98
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:55,358
within a few hours' steaming of a mountain no one had charted.

99
00:05:55,828 --> 00:05:59,308
Some historians believe that's where they met their end.

100
00:05:59,378 --> 00:06:04,140
Eleven years later, in 1929, the mountain finally went on the charts,

101
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:06,358
and the shipping routes changed.

102
00:06:07,248 --> 00:06:09,488
It should have been the end of the story.

103
00:06:09,558 --> 00:06:12,405
It wasn't.

104
00:06:12,475 --> 00:06:14,417
When she was launched in 1958,

105
00:06:14,437 --> 00:06:19,455
the Edmund Fitzgerald was the biggest ship on the Great Lakes, 729 feet long.

106
00:06:21,545 --> 00:06:24,718
At a quarter past two on the afternoon of November 9, 1975, she left Superior,

107
00:06:24,738 --> 00:06:27,788
Wisconsin, loaded with more than 26,000 tons of taconite, iron ore pellets,

108
00:06:27,808 --> 00:06:28,525
bound for Detroit.

109
00:06:35,855 --> 00:06:38,445
Her captain was Ernest McSorley.

110
00:06:38,515 --> 00:06:42,045
Late that afternoon, a second freighter, the Arthur M.

111
00:06:42,115 --> 00:06:45,445
Anderson, joined her for the trip across the lake.

112
00:06:45,515 --> 00:06:48,275
The forecast wasn't unusual for November.

113
00:06:48,345 --> 00:06:53,425
But by that evening, the weather service had issued gale warnings for the whole lake.

114
00:06:53,495 --> 00:06:57,955
So both ships turned north, to shelter along the Canadian shore.

115
00:06:58,025 --> 00:07:00,285
Captain McSorley slowed down.

116
00:07:00,355 --> 00:07:03,079
Another captain heard him say on the radio,

117
00:07:03,099 --> 00:07:06,335
"We're going to try for some lee from Isle Royale."

118
00:07:06,405 --> 00:07:07,745
Isle Royale.

119
00:07:07,815 --> 00:07:11,005
The Bannockburn was last seen 40 miles from it.

120
00:07:11,075 --> 00:07:12,896
And, according to one engineer,

121
00:07:12,916 --> 00:07:17,055
that turn put the Fitzgerald on a course across the north of the lake…

122
00:07:17,125 --> 00:07:20,095
toward the mountain.

123
00:07:20,165 --> 00:07:22,395
So what really sank her?

124
00:07:22,465 --> 00:07:27,075
More than fifty years later, there is still no single answer.

125
00:07:27,145 --> 00:07:29,475
There are four main ones.

126
00:07:29,545 --> 00:07:34,293
The Coast Guard said water got in through the hatch covers on the deck, slowly,

127
00:07:34,313 --> 00:07:36,525
all day, until she lost her buoyancy.

128
00:07:38,315 --> 00:07:43,971
The National Transportation Safety Board said the hatch covers caved in under giant waves,

129
00:07:43,991 --> 00:07:45,295
and she flooded fast.

130
00:07:46,315 --> 00:07:48,385
The shipping companies said she hit the bottom.

131
00:07:48,455 --> 00:07:54,775
They blamed a different shoal, near Caribou Island, at the eastern end of the lake.

132
00:07:54,845 --> 00:07:57,644
Others blame the waves themselves: a set of giant rogue waves,

133
00:07:57,664 --> 00:08:00,234
the ones sailors on Lake Superior call the three sisters,

134
00:08:00,254 --> 00:08:01,825
burying her deck one after another.

135
00:08:06,345 --> 00:08:08,955
And then there's the mountain.

136
00:08:09,025 --> 00:08:12,495
Before you hear the evidence, take a guess.

137
00:08:12,565 --> 00:08:15,735
The waves, the hatches, or the mountain?

138
00:08:15,805 --> 00:08:17,655
Put it in the comments right now.

139
00:08:17,725 --> 00:08:19,335
One word is enough.

140
00:08:19,405 --> 00:08:23,095
Then, at the end, see if you've changed your mind.

141
00:08:23,165 --> 00:08:28,345
And stay with me, because the case against the mountain is stronger than you'd think…

142
00:08:28,415 --> 00:08:33,154
and then there's the one part of the wreck that changes everything.

143
00:08:33,224 --> 00:08:35,363
The engineer's name is Paul Hainault,

144
00:08:35,383 --> 00:08:40,204
a retired professor of mechanical engineering at Michigan Technological University.

145
00:08:40,984 --> 00:08:44,954
His idea began as a class project with his students.

146
00:08:45,024 --> 00:08:49,429
Hainault believes that at about 9:30 on the morning of November 10,

147
00:08:49,449 --> 00:08:52,004
the Fitzgerald ran over Superior Shoal.

148
00:08:53,484 --> 00:08:54,964
Remember the numbers.

149
00:08:55,034 --> 00:08:57,634
The peak: 21 feet down.

150
00:08:57,704 --> 00:09:02,964
The Fitzgerald, fully loaded: about 25 feet deep.

151
00:09:03,034 --> 00:09:05,574
And the lake wasn't sitting still that day.

152
00:09:05,644 --> 00:09:10,274
The storm was rocking the whole of Lake Superior from end to end.

153
00:09:10,344 --> 00:09:16,874
It was a seiche, the same kind of slow slosh that flooded Buffalo in 1844.

154
00:09:16,944 --> 00:09:17,441
That day,

155
00:09:17,461 --> 00:09:23,924
it pushed the water at Sault Ste. Marie 3 feet over the gates of the locks and flooded a city street a foot deep.

156
00:09:26,014 --> 00:09:30,276
Hainault believes that slosh, and the storm waves on top of it,

157
00:09:30,296 --> 00:09:32,994
helped drag the ship down onto the rock.

158
00:09:33,784 --> 00:09:39,684
In his version, the Fitzgerald scraped about 200 feet of her hull across the shoal.

159
00:09:39,754 --> 00:09:42,814
Remember that number too: 200 feet.

160
00:09:42,884 --> 00:09:44,954
It punctured her in the middle.

161
00:09:45,024 --> 00:09:46,324
She didn't stop.

162
00:09:46,394 --> 00:09:47,714
She kept going.

163
00:09:47,784 --> 00:09:51,994
But from that moment, water was getting in.

164
00:09:52,064 --> 00:09:56,743
If that number, 21 feet, just changed the way you see this story,

165
00:09:56,763 --> 00:09:58,984
do two things for me right now.

166
00:09:59,054 --> 00:10:03,244
Tap the like button, and if you haven't yet, subscribe.

167
00:10:03,314 --> 00:10:05,111
For a small channel like this one,

168
00:10:05,131 --> 00:10:10,294
that's the difference between this story reaching a few hundred people or a few hundred thousand.

169
00:10:11,826 --> 00:10:15,536
Here is what we know for certain about the rest of that day.

170
00:10:15,606 --> 00:10:21,656
By early afternoon the wind was back over 50 knots, and snow cut the visibility.

171
00:10:21,726 --> 00:10:26,826
The Anderson, about 16 miles behind, lost sight of the Fitzgerald.

172
00:10:26,896 --> 00:10:30,696
Just after 3:30, Captain McSorley radioed the Anderson.

173
00:10:30,766 --> 00:10:32,716
He had a fence rail down.

174
00:10:32,786 --> 00:10:35,226
Two vents lost or damaged.

175
00:10:35,296 --> 00:10:36,986
And the ship had a list.

176
00:10:37,056 --> 00:10:39,026
She was leaning to one side.

177
00:10:39,096 --> 00:10:41,636
Two of her pumps were running non-stop.

178
00:10:41,706 --> 00:10:43,296
Those details matter.

179
00:10:43,366 --> 00:10:48,436
The shipping companies argued that a fence rail like that would only snap if the ship had bent…

180
00:10:48,506 --> 00:10:52,099
sagging at both ends, with her middle pushed up,

181
00:10:52,119 --> 00:10:55,486
the way a hull bends when it drags over rock.

182
00:10:55,556 --> 00:11:00,046
They meant the shoal near Caribou Island, which she had just passed.

183
00:11:00,116 --> 00:11:03,176
But it's the kind of damage any grounding could cause.

184
00:11:03,246 --> 00:11:06,696
Then the Fitzgerald lost both radars.

185
00:11:06,766 --> 00:11:08,646
She was blind.

186
00:11:08,716 --> 00:11:14,676
The radio beacon at Whitefish Point, the one she needed to find the way in, was off.

187
00:11:14,746 --> 00:11:19,596
Another captain overheard McSorley say, "Don't allow nobody on deck."

188
00:11:19,666 --> 00:11:25,106
Later, McSorley told him it was one of the worst seas he had ever been in.

189
00:11:25,176 --> 00:11:28,546
By evening, the waves were 25 feet high.

190
00:11:28,616 --> 00:11:35,596
At about 6:30, two huge seas hit the Anderson, 30 to 35 feet high.

191
00:11:36,066 --> 00:11:37,996
One buried her rear cabins.

192
00:11:38,066 --> 00:11:41,666
Her captain said those waves kept rolling on…

193
00:11:41,736 --> 00:11:42,846
toward the Fitzgerald.

194
00:11:42,916 --> 00:11:46,386
At ten past seven came the last words.

195
00:11:46,456 --> 00:11:49,966
"We are holding our own."

196
00:11:50,036 --> 00:11:53,727
Then nothing.

197
00:11:53,797 --> 00:11:57,087
Now, the case against the mountain.

198
00:11:57,157 --> 00:11:59,107
And it's a real one.

199
00:11:59,177 --> 00:12:00,587
First, the timing.

200
00:12:00,657 --> 00:12:04,046
If the Fitzgerald hit a mountain at 9:30 in the morning,

201
00:12:04,066 --> 00:12:07,637
why did her captain say nothing about damage for six hours?

202
00:12:08,877 --> 00:12:11,797
29 experienced sailors on board.

203
00:12:11,867 --> 00:12:14,547
Would no one have felt it?

204
00:12:14,617 --> 00:12:17,587
Second, the official investigations.

205
00:12:17,657 --> 00:12:21,721
Both the Coast Guard and the Safety Board looked hard at the evidence,

206
00:12:21,741 --> 00:12:24,637
and both pointed to the hatch covers, not a shoal.

207
00:12:25,527 --> 00:12:27,957
Third, the wreck itself.

208
00:12:28,027 --> 00:12:33,307
The Fitzgerald lies 530 feet down, in two pieces.

209
00:12:33,377 --> 00:12:38,967
In 1994, an expedition photographed the upturned stern in detail.

210
00:12:39,037 --> 00:12:42,226
There was no sign of damage to the bottom of the stern,

211
00:12:42,246 --> 00:12:46,017
the propeller or the rudder that would show she'd hit the bottom.

212
00:12:46,467 --> 00:12:51,717
Divers who checked the shoal near Caribou Island found no fresh scars either.

213
00:12:51,787 --> 00:12:55,237
But Hainault's theory says the damage wasn't at the stern.

214
00:12:55,307 --> 00:12:57,467
It was in the middle.

215
00:12:57,537 --> 00:13:02,007
And the middle of the Edmund Fitzgerald is the one part of her that doesn't exist anymore.

216
00:13:02,077 --> 00:13:07,343
When the Navy surveyed the wreck, the bow section was about 276 feet long,

217
00:13:07,363 --> 00:13:09,057
and the stern about 253.

218
00:13:11,717 --> 00:13:14,267
She was 729 feet long.

219
00:13:14,337 --> 00:13:19,927
Do the sum, and about 200 feet of her middle is missing.

220
00:13:19,997 --> 00:13:21,407
Two hundred feet.

221
00:13:21,477 --> 00:13:24,927
The same length Hainault says scraped across the mountain.

222
00:13:24,997 --> 00:13:26,467
That could be pure coincidence.

223
00:13:26,537 --> 00:13:30,577
A ship that breaks in two is bound to lose its middle.

224
00:13:30,647 --> 00:13:32,643
But between the bow and the stern,

225
00:13:32,663 --> 00:13:36,377
there's only a heap of iron ore pellets and torn steel plating.

226
00:13:36,447 --> 00:13:38,537
If the proof is anywhere…

227
00:13:38,607 --> 00:13:41,903
that's where it is.

228
00:13:41,973 --> 00:13:48,203
Superior Shoal is still out there today, on every chart, and ships steer around it.

229
00:13:48,273 --> 00:13:51,113
Lake trout still climb its slopes.

230
00:13:51,183 --> 00:13:54,423
The Inkerman and the Cerisoles have never been found.

231
00:13:54,493 --> 00:13:58,933
Treasure hunters call them the Holy Grail of Lake Superior.

232
00:13:59,003 --> 00:14:02,083
The Bannockburn has never been found either.

233
00:14:02,153 --> 00:14:04,078
The Fitzgerald has been found,

234
00:14:04,098 --> 00:14:07,643
and no one can dive her without a licence from Ontario.

235
00:14:07,713 --> 00:14:12,873
Break that rule, and the fine can be up to a million Canadian dollars.

236
00:14:12,943 --> 00:14:15,663
It's a grave.

237
00:14:15,733 --> 00:14:16,303
In 1995,

238
00:14:16,323 --> 00:14:22,713
her bell was brought up and replaced with a copy engraved with the names of the 29 men.

239
00:14:24,093 --> 00:14:28,223
The real bell is now in the shipwreck museum at Whitefish Point.

240
00:14:28,293 --> 00:14:34,443
The day after she sank, the Mariners' Church in Detroit rang its bell 29 times.

241
00:14:34,513 --> 00:14:38,133
Once for each man.

242
00:14:38,203 --> 00:14:43,192
If you've sailed Lake Superior, fished it, or your family worked on the lake boats,

243
00:14:43,212 --> 00:14:45,183
I'd really like to hear from you.

244
00:14:45,523 --> 00:14:48,253
Did anyone ever talk about Superior Shoal?

245
00:14:48,323 --> 00:14:49,873
Tell me in the comments.

246
00:14:49,943 --> 00:14:53,838
Some of the best parts of these stories come from people like you,

247
00:14:53,858 --> 00:14:56,923
and the best stories might end up in a future video.

248
00:14:58,257 --> 00:15:02,447
So, did a hidden mountain sink the Edmund Fitzgerald?

249
00:15:02,517 --> 00:15:04,137
Here's the honest answer.

250
00:15:04,207 --> 00:15:08,047
For the two French warships, the mountain is one of the strongest suspects,

251
00:15:08,067 --> 00:15:11,187
and in more than a hundred years nothing has proved it wrong.

252
00:15:13,237 --> 00:15:17,777
For the Bannockburn, it's one possibility among several.

253
00:15:17,847 --> 00:15:22,237
For the Fitzgerald, the official answer is the hatch covers.

254
00:15:22,307 --> 00:15:23,502
The mountain is a theory,

255
00:15:23,522 --> 00:15:29,287
and the only place that could prove it or rule it out is the twisted middle of a wreck that no one is allowed to touch.

256
00:15:31,267 --> 00:15:33,087
But this much is certain.

257
00:15:33,157 --> 00:15:36,008
In the middle of the deepest of the Great Lakes,

258
00:15:36,028 --> 00:15:39,837
there's a mountain that reaches up to 21 feet below the surface.

259
00:15:39,907 --> 00:15:44,767
And for a long time, nobody knew it was there.

260
00:15:44,837 --> 00:15:46,477
So now you've heard all of it.

261
00:15:46,547 --> 00:15:48,587
Do you think the mountain did it?

262
00:15:48,657 --> 00:15:52,377
Tell me in the comments, yes or no, and why.

263
00:15:52,447 --> 00:15:54,927
I read every one.

264
00:15:54,997 --> 00:15:57,492
